  "id": "DrugBank_DB00525 - MONDO_5982",
  "verbalization": "Tolnaftate appears to be linked to Tinea Infection through a series of indications that converge on dermatophytosis, a fungal infection. The evidence suggests that Tolnaftate is used to treat dermatophytosis of the groin and perianal area, which in turn is associated with Tinea Infection. This pathway is supported by multiple high-scoring connections, with Tolnaftate's indication for dermatophytosis of the groin and perianal area being consistently ranked among the top in the dataset. The presence of multiple related agents—such as Sulconazole and Econazole—further reinforces the idea that Tolnaftate may be part of a broader class of treatments for fungal infections, potentially indicating a mechanism of action involving antifungal activity. The integration of these pathways highlights a recurring mechanism of action that involves the treatment of fungal infections, with Tolnaftate serving as a potential bridge between dermatophytosis and Tinea Infection. The evidence suggests that Tolnaftate may be repurposed for use in Tinea Infection, though further studies would be needed to confirm its efficacy and mechanism of action in this context.",
